The Security Council,
Recalling its resolutions 2042 (2012), 2043 (2012), 2118 (2013), 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2175 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2192 (2014), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2268 (2016), 2286 (2016), 2332 (2016), 2336 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018) and 2449 (2018),
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of Syria, and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,
Reaffirming also that all parties to the armed conflict in Syria must comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law, including those relating to the protection of civilians and medical personnel, and the obligation to allow and facilitate rapid and unimpeded passage of humanitarian relief,
Expressing grave concern at the deteriorating humanitarian situation in Syria, including the significant displacement of civilians, and the need for humanitarian assistance to all parts of Syria,
Noting that the humanitarian situation in Syria remains grave and that the continued delivery of humanitarian assistance to all parts of Syria is required,
Reaffirming the need to ensure that humanitarian assistance is delivered in accordance with the humanitarian principles of humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence,
Stressing the need to combat terrorism and to prevent the diversion of humanitarian assistance to terrorist groups,
Recalling that the cross-border mechanism is a temporary measure and that the primary responsibility for the delivery of humanitarian assistance lies with the Syrian authorities,
Welcoming the efforts of the United Nations and its humanitarian partners to deliver humanitarian assistance to all parts of Syria,
1. Decides that the authorization and modalities set out in paragraphs 2 and 3 of resolution 2165 (2014) and renewed by subsequent resolutions shall be renewed for a period of six months, until 10 July 2020, and that the provisions of paragraphs 2 and 3 of resolution 2165 (2014) shall apply to the two border crossings of Bab al-Hawa and Bab al-Salam;
2. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to inform the Security Council, and to report to the Council on the implementation of this resolution, including on the humanitarian situation, the use of the crossings, and any impediments to the delivery of humanitarian assistance, within 60 days, and to provide recommendations on how to improve cross-line deliveries;
3. Emphasizes that the delivery of humanitarian assistance must be in accordance with the humanitarian principles and in coordination with the relevant parties, and that all parties must ensure unhindered access;
4. Calls upon all parties to ensure the safety and security of United Nations and humanitarian personnel, and to facilitate rapid and unimpeded passage of humanitarian relief;
5. Reaffirms that the cross-border mechanism is a temporary measure, and calls for the expansion of cross-line deliveries, including to areas not reached by cross-border operations, and requests the United Nations to intensify efforts to expand cross-line deliveries with the consent of the Syrian authorities;
6. Stresses the need to strengthen the monitoring and reporting mechanism to prevent diversion of humanitarian assistance to terrorist groups, and requests the Secretary-General to include in his report information on measures taken in this regard;
7. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

